---
// Local.astro - the way in for a program that reads no proxy setting at all,
// and the one thing about it that has to be said out loud: what is not chosen
// is never read.
//
// The split drawn below is the design decision, not a diagram of the wiring.
// The operating system routes by destination, and no route can say "this
// program", so what arrives at the adapter is every connection leaving this
// machine; which programs to keep is then a filter tracewing applies to what
// is already its own. Saying only "it captures a program" would leave a
// reader to assume the rest was captured too and merely hidden.
import { CAPTURING_DOC_URL } from '../data/site';
---

<section class="section local" id="local">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-header">
      <span class="eyebrow">Apps that ignore the proxy</span>
      <h2>When there is no proxy setting to set</h2>
      <p>
        A Go binary, a Java program, an installer, a service, an Electron app
        with a network stack of its own: plenty of programs never look at a
        proxy setting, and no amount of setting one will bring them through. So
        tracewing stops asking them, and has the operating system hand their
        connections over instead.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="local-inner">
      <div class="local-text">
        <ul class="check-list">
          <li>
            <strong>Nothing is told anything.</strong> On macOS a network extension
            takes the flows of the apps you chose. On Windows and Linux an adapter
            of tracewing's own, and routes into it, take this machine's traffic. One
            tab and one answer cover both; which machinery is underneath is what nobody
            should have to know.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>One privileged step, once.</strong> The system asks you to allow
            it, and the switch takes effect when it is pressed — no restart, and the
            session open now carries on. On Linux the device stays with its carrier
            down and its routes marked dead, so nothing is sent into it, and the next
            run picks it up without asking again.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>A program with no name of its own is taken by where it is.</strong>{' '}
            Every Go binary <code>go build</code> makes is <code>a.out</code>, and
            what a linker signs changes with each build — so pick the file, or the
            folder it is built into, and it keeps working across rebuilds.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>HTTP/3, for a program you chose.</strong> Its QUIC is terminated
            on the adapter and the server is asked over HTTP/3 too. Off by default:
            a program nobody chose is refused and falls back to HTTP/2 — and that
            refusal is still a row, with the server name it asked for and the program
            that sent it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Every flow says which way in took it</strong>, so a session
            mixing a phone, a terminal and a chosen program is still readable.{' '}
            <a href={CAPTURING_DOC_URL}>What each way in takes</a> is written down.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="split" role="group" aria-label="What the adapter does with each connection">
        <p class="split-top">
          Everything this machine sends arrives at tracewing's own adapter
        </p>
        <div class="split-lanes">
          <article class="lane lane-kept">
            <span class="lane-tag">Kept</span>
            <h3 class="lane-title">The programs you chose</h3>
            <p class="lane-text">
              Decrypted, recorded, paused on a breakpoint, changed by a rule —
              everything the rest of tracewing does.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article class="lane lane-passed">
            <span class="lane-tag">Passed on</span>
            <h3 class="lane-title">Everything else</h3>
            <p class="lane-text">
              Spliced straight through. Its TLS is never terminated and nothing
              of it is stored. There is no capture-everything mode, by design.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <figure class="local-shot">
      <img
        src="/shots/apps.webp"
        width="1600"
        height="1000"
        alt="Settings, Capture, Apps: the tab strip of every way in, with the Apps tab open — what it catches, what it does not, and the two programs it keeps."
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>
        Settings › Capture, where every way in has a tab of its own. Each one
        says what it catches, what it does not, and what has to trust tracewing.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .local {
    background: var(--bg-secondary);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
  }

  .local-inner {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.05fr) minmax(0, 0.95fr);
    gap: 3rem;
    align-items: start;
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }

  .split {
    background: var(--bg-card);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-card);
    border-radius: 16px;
    padding: var(--card-pad);
  }

  .split-top {
    text-align: center;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    padding-bottom: 1.25rem;
    position: relative;
  }

  /* The one line in, splitting into the two lanes under it. */
  .split-top::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: 25%;
    right: 25%;
    bottom: 0;
    height: 0.75rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--border-card);
    border-bottom: 0;
    border-radius: 6px 6px 0 0;
  }

  .split-lanes {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 1rem;
    padding-top: 0.75rem;
  }

  .lane {
    padding: var(--inset-pad);
    border-radius: 12px;
    border: 1px solid var(--border-card);
    background: var(--bg-inset);
  }

  .lane-kept {
    border-color: rgba(122, 162, 255, 0.4);
    background: rgba(122, 162, 255, 0.08);
  }

  .lane-tag {
    display: inline-block;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .lane-kept .lane-tag {
    color: var(--accent-light);
  }

  .lane-passed .lane-tag {
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .lane-title {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
  }

  .lane-passed .lane-title {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  .lane-text {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
  }

  .local-shot {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .local-shot img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    border: 1px solid var(--border-card);
    border-radius: 16px;
    box-shadow: 0 30px 60px -20px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.7);
  }

  .local-shot figcaption {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    text-align: center;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 60rem) {
    .local-inner {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 2rem;
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 34rem) {
    .split-lanes {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .split-top::after {
      left: 50%;
      right: auto;
      width: 1px;
      border-radius: 0;
      border-width: 0 0 0 1px;
    }
  }
</style>
